{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Behold, My servant shall prosper.</b> This pericope is very difficult. Those who err in spirit said that it alludes to Elohim, and they interpret 'my servant' as the body. This is impossible, for the body does not prosper [understand] even when a person is alive. Furthermore, what is the reason [for] 'he shall see seed' — 'he shall prolong days'? And he had no son. Furthermore, 'he shall prolong days' — and this was not so. Furthermore, 'and he shall divide the spoil with the strong' — and the proof is complete that it refers to what came before, for 'he who goes before you [is] YHWH,' and they are Israel, and afterward, 'Sing, O barren one' — and she is the congregation of Israel. Now the meaning of 'my servant' is everyone from Israel who was in exile and is a servant of HaShem. And many interpreted it of the messiah, because our ancestors of blessed memory said [Midrash Rabbah Lamentations 1:16] that on the day the Temple was destroyed, the messiah was born, and he is bound in chains. Now there is no meaning to many verses: 'despised and shunning men,' 'taken from prison and from judgment,' 'and they made his grave with the wicked' — and what is the reason [for] 'he shall see seed, he shall prolong days'? And the Gaon [Saadia], Rav Saadia of blessed memory, interpreted the entire pericope of Jeremiah, and he interpreted well. And the meaning of 'he shall startle many nations' [is] by way of his prophecy. 'A suckling before him' — for he wrote at the beginning of his book (Jeremiah 1:10) that he was young when he prophesied. 'And YHWH interceded in him' — and 'he bore the sin of many,' for thus it is written: 'stood before You to seek good on their behalf' (Jeremiah 18:20). 'Like a lamb led to slaughter' — and thus it is written: 'and I, like a trusting lamb, am led to slaughter' (Jeremiah 11:19). And he interpreted 'and he shall divide the spoil with the strong' as the portion and gift that the chief of the guard gave him (Jeremiah 40:5). Now what seems correct to me is that the entire pericope is connected, for what reason would there be to mention Jeremiah after the consolations and before the consolations? Rather, it speaks of every servant of YHWH who is in exile. Or 'my servant' may be like 'Israel, My servant,' and this is close to that:" "And now <b>shall prosper ['understand,' ישכיל].</b> [It means] 'my servant shall understand,' for 'yet he shall be exalted and lifted up' [is from] the nif'al binyan."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 52, :notes {0 "The epithet הגאון is resolved as Saadia by the immediately following words 'Rav Saadia of blessed memory.' The phrase והם ישראל is syntactically elliptical; translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:59:21.432649805Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}